#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634} :activity/id #uuid "4417fe29-8f51-5120-ac66-9aaeadadb6f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075034 :tutorial/content "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5,$ for example, we get the ratio $10:30.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  \\\\ \\,10 &  & : &  & \\,30 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent. \n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2,$ we get $1:3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios  $2:6$ and  $1:3$ are also equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc2c9ce9-c2b0-5d68-81db-b8b0273407f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6aa20fa4-3163-5025-84f7-a1ddd4324cd0" :step/math-academy-id 186681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3539d419-9d5a-5467-8a88-7ef318329859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8204 :tutorial/title "Finding Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "4854b84c-59a0-5a98-a9c6-9b8427de8ab8" :step/math-academy-id 186682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073447 :question/id #uuid "a453e80d-016f-5a6c-9890-705463eb1fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ac1e54-c949-5ffe-b377-b0e35240a13c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518125}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "55be2711-44d0-588a-899f-562ac0853236" :step/math-academy-id 186683 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075036 :tutorial/content "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio: \n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9,$ so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1.$ \n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ece136-e063-594d-b25d-4cf19bca7890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8205 :tutorial/title "Expressing Ratios in Their Simplest Form"} :step/id #uuid "23872027-241e-5f61-8492-76236e405360" :step/math-academy-id 186686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073448 :question/id #uuid "0a57949c-efc9-5403-9e7b-08346153f184" :question/math-academy-id "e-13121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae17eb3f-f444-5ab3-82fd-6c92b97b0e41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515552}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "092ffde3-3422-5950-9ca5-9b76fcb363ff" :step/math-academy-id 186687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073449 :question/id #uuid "dd86c5a5-b8be-5d19-a477-dcf46683881f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d16202af-d3ac-54bc-9c76-25947a7a0b60" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518216}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement"} :step/id #uuid "36a366b2-6119-57ed-87f3-fd57834dc2d6" :step/math-academy-id 186690}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073972}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490342} :activity/id #uuid "34509cbd-152a-5cd3-b3bc-231dc0557f02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f524f-5dec-5e52-ac4c-9fb446367ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} {:answer/id #uuid "640e11a1-45f1-5bd9-ba70-d2bd9afff6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489750} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8d1a24-f0b3-5957-bac1-4b551d6b4aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489770} {:answer/id #uuid "736365bf-c5ec-516a-96d3-5dcf4aad4bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489785} {:answer/id #uuid "8790ecbf-d319-56cd-88f6-19ef7c84fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7379773-3775-53b0-a3c8-4af0a853a08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ddf5db22-7c12-5596-b4f6-5a61a8187c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-59828" :question/problem "If $10$ children ate $15$ cakes, what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $3$ eaten cakes for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ children." :question/worked-solution "Since $10$ children can eat $15$ cakes, the ratio of cakes to children is $15:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:2\\,$ because there are $3$ cakes for every $2\\,$ children.\"\n\nThe missing number is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "2b306b5b-d4d0-5bd9-ad85-47853cadb9b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab0f3fb-4e36-5cb7-9b92-bf6665005eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489538} {:answer/id #uuid "4b95827d-b1f3-513f-a04b-4e3930a7a8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489690} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c18ff-74f1-5d6b-965e-9c4142e35106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489857} {:answer/id #uuid "a3480f70-f3e4-5b52-9b49-7446ba7d2f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} {:answer/id #uuid "c8575184-e89c-5e39-8b80-accc5525b223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e569b9-68b5-54d0-bd0f-496bb3345979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec0c1b9c-5490-5482-a264-c0fe31855e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-101444" :question/problem "![](images/ed/eda2132e8e050399298b97dc9d36cf27862d6aa4625e7aa870bb4c8fd246e781.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the number of pentagons?" :question/worked-solution "We count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nWe count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $4$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $2:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $1:2.$"} :step/id #uuid "338c847b-f4c7-5b56-ae4c-a5bc33c176b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8636ec-a6ca-5436-8dd7-6da7ca001b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489611} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f1e389-8ab5-503e-b205-cc5f0bcc7461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489916} {:answer/id #uuid "c459ed53-041f-5cf1-a8ab-49b000eb425c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489940} {:answer/id #uuid "cd10f7eb-7f59-5705-9648-668ef168f8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ee81e2-8914-5cd6-8aba-c7a5b8a811e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa08328e-cc55-5205-a1b2-f02f8b69f734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1816745e-33d2-5605-bad0-d9ea4d6d5feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-59896" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6c5263a440942da90d5ed6dc6a87e9e6caa0848dfb23c1a904eb828dfcaaeb1.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $\\boxed{}$ pentagons." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $2$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $2$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $2:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $2:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $1$ pentagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "f85a5132-bc8d-5028-bf7c-c2b4e0758ecd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489503}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630} :activity/id #uuid "fbfe7b63-dd84-5bb9-ae0b-151a90820b22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf06951-cbcc-525c-9842-0890581834c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072477} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ecb02-3d9b-5237-9855-274124892856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072478} {:answer/id #uuid "d7352e89-2b19-5a8c-a0e2-f926cfe67ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf88531-0b03-5971-a795-d3362dd0c20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072480} {:answer/id #uuid "41370d63-4b05-5808-abe3-4ce7f528b248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad60804-e649-58c3-9918-6d89f3cb7178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd08e83a-7cd3-5ff7-9cbf-8fd4782df22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-59740" :question/problem "![](images/38/3865ec425991f0d8ceae82f002d0096f2fd2ea56743173e754b15472b7c85031.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}$ hexagon." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $3$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $3$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $1$ hexagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "11c74a03-1f27-5d00-94fb-dfb867dd0243" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515529 :question/answer-fi